  "subtitle": "the first mainstream speech synthesizer you could buy — a beige box that gave Stephen Hawking his voice and read phone books aloud at 300 words per minute",
  "why_scout_cares": "Before text-to-speech came bundled in every phone and screen reader, there was a dedicated beige box that turned typed text into spoken words in real time: the DECtalk. Designed by Dennis Klatt at MIT and the Speech Technology Group at Digital Equipment Corporation, the DCT01 (1984) was the first commercial formant-synthesis TTS product, sold to labs, accessibility users, and telephone services. It defined the orthography and voice quality of computer speech for a generation, and — in the specific voice of its 'Perfect Paul' preset — became Stephen Hawking's signature voice from 1986 until the end. Klatt's cascade formant-synthesis model, developed in the Klatt Synthesizer (KlattTalk), remains one of the most influential accounts of how to reproduce speech acoustically. DECtalk is the museum's purest artifact of machines-that-speak: an interface whose entire purpose is to make text audible, and whose flaws and character were so distinctive they became part of a person's identity.",
  "search_terms": ["DECtalk DCT01", "DECtalk", "Dennis Klatt KlattTalk", "Klatt synthesizer", "Perfect Paul voice", "Stephen Hawking speech synthesizer", "formant synthesis TTS"],
  "research": {
    "title": "DECtalk DCT01",
